WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:02.676
(audience cheers)

00:00:02.676 --> 00:00:03.840
- Hello!

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.310
Well, hello and meet Kristin.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:06.210
Have y'all ever met before?
- No.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.430
- No, but I've seen him on stage and what,

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.250
your acting, you're such an artist.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.288
You are so good.

00:00:12.288 --> 00:00:13.121
(audience cheers)

00:00:13.121 --> 00:00:14.021
- Thank you.
- I'm not lying.

00:00:14.021 --> 00:00:15.054
She was talking about you
in the commercial break.

00:00:15.054 --> 00:00:15.897
- Yeah?
- She was like,

00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:16.730
"I'm so excited to meet him.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:17.760
I'm so excited to come
out, for him to come out."

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:18.593
- Couldn't wait.

00:00:18.593 --> 00:00:19.500
- Yes, I'm excited to meet you, too.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.385
Actually, the other
day I was watching RV--

00:00:24.385 --> 00:00:26.166
- Oh my God, I love.

00:00:26.166 --> 00:00:26.999
- Oh my God, you saw that.

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:27.832
- And was laughing hysterically,

00:00:27.832 --> 00:00:28.665
so it's, it's an honor.

00:00:28.665 --> 00:00:30.447
- Thank you, honey.
- Yes.

00:00:30.447 --> 00:00:31.448
- Thank you.
- Yes, yes.

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:32.281
And your theater legacy is incredible.

00:00:32.281 --> 00:00:33.493
Thank you.

00:00:33.493 --> 00:00:34.590
- That means a lot to me.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:35.550
- Yeah.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.070
Well, like Kristin, you
have also been on Broadway.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.260
So it was actually the first time

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.690
Death of a Salesman had an all
African American cast, right?

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:43.523
- Yes, yes.

00:00:43.523 --> 00:00:44.430
I mean.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:45.960
- [Kelly] Did that feel like pressure?

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.040
- It felt like a lot of pressure.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.810
I mean, this is a play,

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:49.710
it's one of the greatest

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.440
American plays ever--
- Ever.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.520
- To be written.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.264
- [Kristin] And your role--

00:00:55.264 --> 00:00:56.190
- And my role as Biff is something

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.820
that a young actor just
dreams about being able to do,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.620
let alone on Broadway.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.870
So when we were gonna do the play,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.060
first, you have to understand
this play has been done

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.010
so many times.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:08.880
- 'Cause it's classic and great.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:09.713
- It's classic and great.

00:01:09.713 --> 00:01:12.258
So you've either read it,
you've either seen it,

00:01:12.258 --> 00:01:13.590
you have either done it in class,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.600
so you have your own
perspective on it, right?

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.650
So how do you do it new?

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.110
How do you change someone's
perspective on something

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.108
that's been done so well so many times?

00:01:21.108 --> 00:01:21.941
- [Kristin] Right.

00:01:21.941 --> 00:01:22.774
- And then on top of that,

00:01:22.774 --> 00:01:24.330
it's being done with an all black cast,

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.857
so, then you have to have the question of,

00:01:26.857 --> 00:01:28.620
"Is it going to resonate?

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.690
Will the language read the same way?"

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.700
So there was that pressure,

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.180
but I think what we all did with our show,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.190
we didn't change any of the dialogue;

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.160
the script stayed the same,
but the nuances changed.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:41.993
- Oh, yeah.

00:01:41.993 --> 00:01:42.826
- You understand?
- Yes.

00:01:42.826 --> 00:01:46.860
- So we all dug in, we gave
it everything that we had.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.420
We gave our hearts, we gave our spirits.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.070
And that's all you can ask someone to do

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.569
when they're on that stage.

00:01:51.569 --> 00:01:52.402
(audience applauds)

00:01:52.402 --> 00:01:54.757
- Yeah.
- Yes!

00:01:54.757 --> 00:01:57.180
- And young black actors, now they see

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.150
that they can broaden the
scope on the kind of work

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.950
that they can do and
that they're able to do.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.230
As long as the story, I believe,

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.600
speaks to our humanity on a deep level,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.040
we'll all be able to understand it

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.930
no matter who's telling the story.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.400
- Absolutely.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.233
Nice.

00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:13.066
I know, I'm like, gosh.

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:14.123
- So well spoken.

00:02:14.123 --> 00:02:14.956
Right.

00:02:14.956 --> 00:02:15.990
- Well you also, did I hear that you,

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:16.823
oh I got you, girl.

00:02:16.823 --> 00:02:17.880
I cry all the time on this show.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.713
Check it out.

00:02:18.713 --> 00:02:19.825
- Sorry.
- Yep, yep.

00:02:19.825 --> 00:02:20.799
They got a whole load
of 'em over here for me.

00:02:20.799 --> 00:02:21.632
- It's just so well said.

00:02:21.632 --> 00:02:22.465
- I know, I know.

00:02:22.465 --> 00:02:24.630
So the new movie is
called Big George Foreman,

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:25.950
and it's about the life of, obviously,

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.467
former heavyweight champ, right?

00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:28.300
- Yes.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.133
Yes, it is.

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:30.120
- You like to take on some roles

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.500
that come with some like
pressure, like the bar is--

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.790
- [Khris] I do, I do.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.264
- Do you do that on purpose
to challenge yourself?

00:02:35.264 --> 00:02:36.097
- I do.

00:02:36.097 --> 00:02:36.930
Yes.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:37.763
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:02:37.763 --> 00:02:38.760
- Because I feel for myself

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.283
that telling stories is a purpose, right?

00:02:42.154 --> 00:02:45.270
And acting for me tends to be spiritual.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.103
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:46.103 --> 00:02:48.886
- So when I connect to a role,

00:02:48.886 --> 00:02:50.490
it's often instinctual

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.230
'cause I can feel a call to it,

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.060
I can feel a purpose to it, right?

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.740
So taking on this story

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.310
and telling the legacy
of Mr. Foreman, right,

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:03.696
from younger years all the way into 45.

00:03:03.696 --> 00:03:04.529
- Yeah.

00:03:04.529 --> 00:03:06.000
- I felt like there were
a lot of layers there.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.931
There were a lot of--
- A lot of life there.

00:03:07.931 --> 00:03:08.764
- Complexities and detail.

00:03:08.764 --> 00:03:09.597
It's a lot of life there,

00:03:09.597 --> 00:03:11.850
so there's a lot of things that
you can do with that, right?

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.040
Life change, I mean, year by year.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.420
So, decade by decade.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.590
So much happens.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.540
- Was it hard to go from the young to old?

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.790
Like obviously the transformation of that?

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.160
- It was difficult to do that,

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.840
but the hardest part of that was

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.560
the weight transitions.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.180
So we shot out of sequence, meaning

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.520
one week I could be 19

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.610
and then four days later, I have to be 32.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.850
Which means, at 32, he's
a heavyweight champion.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.620
At 19, he's not.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.540
So I would have to shift my weight.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.460
So week by week.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.470
Yeah, that's, I put that weight on.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:54.249
So, week by week, I would
go from being 225 pounds.

00:03:54.249 --> 00:03:55.082
- [Kelly] Wait, what?

00:03:55.082 --> 00:03:55.915
Wait.
- Yeah.

00:03:55.915 --> 00:03:56.850
- No, no, no, no.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:57.683
- [Khris] Wanna see it again?

00:03:57.683 --> 00:03:58.516
- No, wait.

00:03:58.516 --> 00:03:59.349
What do you mean?

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:00.182
Wait, somebody put that back up there.

00:04:00.182 --> 00:04:01.015
Wait, what?

00:04:01.015 --> 00:04:01.848
- Yeah.

00:04:01.848 --> 00:04:02.681
- What do you mean?

00:04:02.681 --> 00:04:03.531
That's you?

00:04:03.531 --> 00:04:04.364
- That's me.

00:04:04.364 --> 00:04:05.197
- That--
- A hundred percent.

00:04:05.197 --> 00:04:06.480
- That's crazy.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.082
That's awesome.

00:04:08.082 --> 00:04:10.140
(audience applauds)

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:10.973
Awesome.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.013
- Yeah.

00:04:13.013 --> 00:04:13.846
- I want that part.

00:04:13.846 --> 00:04:15.000
- I don't even know how to follow that.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.350
Like that is incredible.
- I know.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.760
- Like that's a lot of,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.040
that's a lot on your body
to go back and forth.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.060
Like that had to be--
- That's skill.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.080
- How did you do that?

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.290
I mean, well, I can yo-yo
quite well myself, but like,

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.540
but like, I just mean like
that's a lot different.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.770
- In a quick amount of time.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.720
- [Kelly] That looks
like a different a human.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.720
- So we finished the first block,

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.030
and I say first block 'cause
we filmed in two blocks,

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.793
and we took six weeks off
for me to gain the weight.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.070
And like I said, I was
vacillating between 225 and 242,

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.510
depending on the age.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:46.560
And that was week by week.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.360
So one week I'd be 242

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.070
for all the heavyweight
championship fights

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:51.150
and then I would cut down

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.180
to about 228, 225 to
play the younger years.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.908
And that was happening quite frequently.

00:04:56.908 --> 00:04:57.741
- Oh my gosh.

00:04:57.741 --> 00:05:00.180
- And we were filming like
five scenes a day sometimes.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.838
So there were some days where
I couldn't cut down to 225.

00:05:02.838 --> 00:05:03.671
- [Kelly] That's crazy, physically.

00:05:03.671 --> 00:05:04.504
- Right.

00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:05.610
So then it's like, how do I shrink myself?

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.173
'Cause I'm out there and I'm swole.

00:05:08.173 --> 00:05:09.006
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:09.006 --> 00:05:11.280
- But I shrink myself to
be, you know, younger.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.793
- So wait, then you took six
weeks off and that's when they?

00:05:13.793 --> 00:05:15.000
- I took six weeks off, right.
- Oh, man.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.060
- And I had a nutritionist and yeah,

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.080
they gave me a plan.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.390
- How long did it take you to drop that?

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.710
- Well,

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.530
I was back down to 240
in about four weeks.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.320
- Man, you have a very responsive body.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.479
- Well--
- Like what?

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:31.440
It listens.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.550
- I think because I had
trained for so long that

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.830
I put the weight on so fast,

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.350
that my body was ready to let it go.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:44.418
So once I stopped eating
7,000 calories a day--

00:05:44.418 --> 00:05:45.312
- Yeah, that's a, yeah--

00:05:45.312 --> 00:05:46.145
- My body was like, all right, we're good,

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:47.784
we're good, we're good,
we're good, we're good.

00:05:47.784 --> 00:05:48.750
And I had to--

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.185
- But it's like training
your stomach to not,

00:05:50.185 --> 00:05:51.546
to shrink back, you know, too, yeah.

00:05:51.546 --> 00:05:52.612
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:52.612 --> 00:05:53.445
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:53.445 --> 00:05:55.290
That was the hardest part.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.056
It just didn't wanna go anywhere.

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:57.889
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:57.889 --> 00:05:58.722
- It just didn't wanna go anywhere.

00:05:58.722 --> 00:05:59.700
- Yeah, I feel ya.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.640
- All right, we have to
stop for another break.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.170
But everybody, Big George Foreman

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.033
is in theaters starting April 28th.

